const notes = [
{
id: 1,
content: 'HTML is easy',
important: true
},
{
id: 2,
content: 'Browser can execute only JavaScript',
important: false
},
{
id: 3,
content: 'GET and POST are the most important methods of HTTP protocol',
important: true
}
]

ReactDOM.createRoot(document.getElementById('root')).render(
<App notes={notes} />
)

The way way rendered the list above is due to the limited elements in the array but they were many items in the array, it would be feasible
A single note is rendered by accesssing thhe objects in the array by referring to a hard-coded index number:

<li>{notes[1].content}</li>

<!-- So we can improve on this by generate React elements from the array objects using the map function -->

<!-- returns every item as a list  -->

notes.map(note=> <li>{note.content}</li>)

<!-- This is the result -->
<!-- This is how the new array is returned  -->

[

  <li>HTML is easy</li>,
  <li>Browser can execute only JavaScript</li>,
  <li>GET and POST are the most important methods of HTTP protocol</li>,
]

Now these lists can be placed inside ul tags

<!-- App component -->

const App = (props) => {
const { notes } = props

return (

<div>
<h1>Notes</h1>
<ul>
{notes.map(note => <li>{note.content}</li>)}
</ul>
</div>
)
}

Key-attribute
The elements generated by the map method, must each have a unique key value: an attribute called "Key"
So lets add the keys !!

const App = (props) => {
const { notes } = props

return (

<div>
<h1>Notes</h1>
<ul>
{notes.map(note =>
<!-- The key attribute is added -->
<li key={note.id}>
{note.content}
</li>
)}
</ul>
</div>
)
}

React uses the key attributes of objects in an array to determine how to update the view generated by a component when the component is re-rendered !!

Map - Understanding how the array method ("map") works is crucial for this course

<!-- An array of objects called notes, at each index of the array, there is an object -->

const notes = [
{
id: 1,
content: 'HTML is easy',
important: true
},
{
id: 2,
content: 'Browser can execute only JavaScript',
important: false
},
{
id: 3,
content: 'GET and POST are the most important methods of HTTP protocol',
important: true
}
]

<!-- step by step, we breaking down how the map method works -->

const result = notes.map(note => note.id)
console.log(result)
[1,2,3] will be printed to the console.
Map always creates a new array, the elements of which have been created from the elements of the original array by mapping: using the function given as a parameter to the map method

The function is (Its a callback function because it will be passed into another function)

note => note.id
or
(note) => {
return note.id
}

ANTI-PATTERN: ARRAY INDEXES AS KEYS
When we are using arrays, we can use the index of the array  as "keys" to stop 
the index error message in react!!
- You pass the index as a second parameter to the callback function of the 
map method 

<!-- The i, is the index of the element in the array, and the note is the element itself -->
notes.map((note, i) => ...)

<!-- PUTTING IT ALL TOGETHER  -->
 <ul>
  {notes.map((note, i) => 
    <li key={i}>
      {note.content}
    </li>
  )}
</ul>

This however, not recommended and can create undesired problems even if it seems to be working 
just fine !!

REFACTORING MODULES 
Tidy our code up a bit, mostly using props and destructing them

<!-- The app component takes in a prop which is an array of object  -->
const App = ({ notes }) => {
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Notes</h1>
      <ul>
        <!-- Now lets render a collection using the map method, an array of objects  -->
        {notes.map(note => 
          <li key={note.id}>
            {note.content}
          </li>
        )}
      </ul>
    </div>
  )
}

<!-- MOre refactoring -->
<!-- Note component takes in a prop, which we have already destructred -->
const Note = ({ note }) => {
  return (
    <li>{note.content}</li>
  )
}

const App = ({ notes }) => {
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Notes</h1>
      <ul>
        {notes.map(note => 
          <!-- using the component, notes -->
           <!-- We now specify the key attribute when using the component itsef not it in the initial definition of the component or not for the li tags like before -->
          <Note key={note.id} note={note} />
        )}
      </ul>
    </div>
  )
}

A WHOLE REACT APP CAN BE WRITTEN IN A SINGLE FILE. ALTHOUGH THAT'S OF COURSE, NOT VERY PRACTICAL
COMMON PRACTICE IS TO DECLARE EACH COMPONENT IN ITS OWN FILE AS AN ES6-MODULE 

using SEPARATE MODULES, THEN BRING IT ALL TOGETHER !! 

Lets move our NOTE component into its own module,
In smaller applications, components are usually placed in a directory called components, 
which is in turn placed within the src directory. 
<!-- THE CONVENTION IS TO NAME THE FILE AFTER THE COMPONENT -->

<!-- FIRST FILE/MODULE  -->
const Note = ({ note }) => {
  return <li>{note.content}</li>
}

export default Note

The last line of the module exports the declared module, the variable NOTE
 It's just to indicate that we will use the file some where else 

 <!-- NOW THE FILE THAT IS USING THE COMPONENT FROM THE OTHER FILE CAN IMPORT THE MODULE -->
 import Note from './components/Note'

const App = ({ notes }) => {
  // ...
}

MODULES HAVE PLENTY OTHER USES OTHER THAN ENABLING COMPONENT DECLARATIONS TO BE SEPARATED INTO THEIR OWN FILES

WHEN THE APPLICATION BREAKS 
EVEN AFTER MANY YEARS OF CODING, what happens even after 30 years, is that the application  
just completely breaks down. Mostly the case with dynamically typed languages, such as JS 
WHERE THE COMPILER DOESNT CHECK THE DATA TYPE, for instance, function variables or return values 

# REACT EXPLOSION - Can be an error where there is nothing show on the screen, the app just completely breaks 
# IN SUCH SITUTATIONS, THE BEST WAY OUT IS TO CONSOLE.LOG () METHOD 

You can always write the console.log method in a component to see which parts of the component work and those that dont work.

# QUITE OFTEN THE ROOT OF THE PROBLEM IS THAT THE PROPS ARE EXPECTED TO BE OF A DIFFERENT TYPE, OR CALLED WITH 
# DIFFERENT NAME THAN THEY ACTUALLY HAVE, DESTRUCTING FAILS AS A RESULT. 
# THE PROBLEM OFTE BEGINS TO SOLVE ITSELF WHEN DESTRUCTING IS REMOVED AND WE SEE WHAT THE PROPS CONTAIN 
# If the problem has still not been resolved, sadly there isn't much to do apart from continuing to bug-hunt by sprinkling more console.log statements around your code.
WHEN THE PROPS ARE OF A WRONG TYPE, THATS WHERE THE PROBLEM IS, SO DEBUG USING console.log

THIS IS AN ANSWER FOR PART 1 , OF THE ANECDOTE EXERCISE 
I SOLVED IT USING AN OBJECT  !!!!
import React from "react";

import { useState } from "react";

const App = () => {
  const anecdotes = [
    "If it hurts, do it more often.",
    "Adding manpower to a late software project makes it later!",
    "The first 90 percent of the code accounts for the first 90 percent of the development time...The remaining 10 percent of the code accounts for the other 90 percent of the development time.",
    "Any fool can write code that a computer can understand. Good programmers write code that humans can understand.",
    "Premature optimization is the root of all evil.",
    "Debugging is twice as hard as writing the code in the first place. Therefore, if you write the code as cleverly as possible, you are, by definition, not smart enough to debug it.",
    "Programming without an extremely heavy use of console.log is same as if a doctor would refuse to use x-rays or blood tests when diagnosing patients.",
    "The only way to go fast, is to go well.",
  ];

  const [selected, setSelected] = useState(0);
  const [votes, setVotes] = useState(Array(anecdotes.length).fill(0));
  const [mostVotes, setMostVotes] = useState(0);

  const handleRandom = () => {
    const randomNumber = Math.floor(Math.random() * anecdotes.length);
    setSelected(randomNumber);
  };

  const handleVotes = () => {
    setVotes({
      ...votes,
      [selected]: votes[selected] + 1,
    });

    console.log("Votes after update", votes);
  };

  // get the property with the most votes in the object
  for (let key in votes) {
    console.log("most votes before", mostVotes);
    if (votes[key] > mostVotes) {
      setMostVotes(votes[key]);
    }
    console.log("most votes after", mostVotes);
  }
  // FIND THAT QUOTE WITH THE MOST VOTES
  const key = Object.keys(votes).find((key) => votes[key] == mostVotes);
  return (
    <>
      <div>
        <Header text="Anecdotes of the Day" />
        <div>{anecdotes[selected]}</div>
        <div> has {votes[selected]} votes</div>
        <button onClick={handleVotes}>vote</button>
        <button onClick={handleRandom}>next anecdote</button>
      </div>

      {/* Display the quote with the most votes */}
      <MostVoted mostVotes={mostVotes} quote={anecdotes[key]} />
    </>
  );
};

// component to conditionally rendered the quote with the most votes,
// if zero is the most, dont display any quote
const MostVoted = ({ mostVotes, quote }) => {
  if (mostVotes === 0) return <div></div>;
  return (
    <div>
      <Header text="Anecdotes with the most votes" />
      <div>{quote}</div>
      <div>has {mostVotes} votes</div>
    </div>
  );
};

// Header component
const Header = ({ text }) => <h3>{text}</h3>;
export default App;

