LETS STICK WITH THE FIRST ONE FOR PROPS 

const App = (props) => {
  <!-- STATE TO TRACK NOTES, AND ANY CHANGES TO IT -->
  const [notes, setNotes] = useState(props.notes)


  <!--HANDLER FUNCTION THAT HANDLES THE ADDITION OF A NEW NOTE -->
  <!-- FUNCTION THAT WILL BE CALLED WHEN THE FORM IS SUBMITTED, BY CLICKING 
   THE SUBMIT BUTTON -->
  const addNote = (event) => {
    event.preventDefault()
    console.log('button clicked', event.target)
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Notes</h1>
      <ul>
        {notes.map(note => 
          <Note key={note.id} note={note} />
        )}
      </ul>

      <!-- The event handler here is submission -->
      <form onSubmit={addNote}>
        <input />
        <button type="submit">save</button>
      </form>   
    </div>
  )
}

// THE FUNCTION TO ADD THE NOTE TO THE NOTES ARRAY IN DETAIL

<!-- ADD NOTE FUNCTION -->
const addNote = (event) => {
  <!-- prevent the form to reload -->
  event.preventDefault()
  console.log('button clicked', event.target)
}

Event Parameter-> is the event that triggers the call to the event handler function.

THE EVENT HANDLER immediately calls event.preventDefault() method, which prevents 
the default action of submitting a form.  THE DEFAULT ACTION, WOULD CAUSE THE PAGE TO RELOAD. 

The target of the event stored in event.target is logged to the console. 
THE target in this case iss the form that we have defined in our component 

HOW DO WE ACCESS THE DATA CONTAINED IN THE FORM'S INPUT ELEMENT ??

--- "WE USE CONTROLLED COMPONENTS" ---  

There are many ways  to accomplish this: 
I) THE FIRST METHOD, use controlled components 

CONTROLLED COMPONENTS: 
lets add a new piece of state called newNote for storing the user-submitted 
input and let's set it as the input element's value attribute;

const App = (props) => {

  <!-- state for the notes -->
  const [notes, setNotes] = useState(props.notes)
 
  <!-- new state for the new note the user wants to add  -->
  const [newNote, setNewNote] = useState(
    'a new note...'
  ) 

  <!-- handler function for the submissio of the form -->
  const addNote = (event) => {
    event.preventDefault()
    console.log('button clicked', event.target)
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Notes</h1>
      <ul>
        {notes.map(note => 
          <Note key={note.id} note={note} />
        )}
      </ul>
      <form onSubmit={addNote}>
       
      <!-- We use the state newNote to set the value of the input element -->
       <!-- Where the user will type into to add the note  -->
        <input value={newNote} />
        <button type="submit">save</button>
      </form>   
    </div>
  )
}

The placeholder text sotred as the initial value of the newNote state appears in the input element,
BUT THE INPUT TEXT can't be edited... 

Since we assigned a piece of the App's component's state as the value Attribute of the input element,
the App component now CONTROLS the behaviour of the input element 

<!-- TO ENABLE EDITING of the input element, WE HAVE TO REGISTER an event handler that 
 synchronizes the changes made to the input with the component's state -->

 const App = (props) => {
  const [notes, setNotes] = useState(props.notes)
  const [newNote, setNewNote] = useState(
    'a new note...'
  ) 

  // ...

<!-- Register the handler function to the onChange event of the input element -->
 <!-- What happens when the user changes the text in the input element? -->
  const handleNoteChange = (event) => {
    console.log(event.target.value)
    <!-- Asses the value of the input element and update the state -->
    setNewNote(event.target.value)
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Notes</h1>
      <ul>
        {notes.map(note => 
          <Note key={note.id} note={note} />
        )}
      </ul>
      <form onSubmit={addNote}>
        <input
          value={newNote}
          <!-- pass in the onChange attribute in the input element  -->
          onChange={handleNoteChange}
        />
        <button type="submit">save</button>
      </form>   
    </div>
  )
}

We have now registered an event handler to the onChange attribute of the form's input 
ELEMENT:::: 

<!-- The input element that dictates what happens when the user changes  -->
<input
  value={newNote}
  onChange={handleNoteChange}
/>

THe event handler is called every time a change occurs in the input element. 
THE EVENT HANDLER FUNCTION RECEIVES THE EVENT OBJECT AS ITS EVENT PARAMETER 

const handleNoteChange = (event) => {
  console.log(event.target.value)
  setNewNote(event.target.value)
}

The target property of this event object now corresponds to the controlled 
INPUT ELEMENT, and event.target.value refers to the input value of that element 

WE DONT NEED to call the event.preventDefault() method like we did in the onSubmit event handler 
BECAUSE NO DEFAULT ACTION OCCURS ON AN INPUT CHANGE, UNLIKE A FORM SUBMISSION

<!-- NOW LETS COMPELETE THE addNote FUNCTION for creating new notes -->
 <!-- THIS IS BECAUSE WE NOW HAVE A FUNCTION THAT TRACKS AND STORES THE CHANGES THAT THE USER  TYPES INTO -->

 NOW ITS TIME TO SUBMIT OR ADD THE NEW NOTE WHEN THE SUBMISSION IS DONE 

 const addNote = (event) => {
  <!-- Prevents the default action of the FORM -->
  event.preventDefault()
  <!-- Creates a new note object -->
  const noteObject = {
    content: newNote,
    important: Math.random() < 0.5,
    id: String(notes.length + 1),
  }
 <!-- CHANGE THE STATE OF THE ARRAY SINCE WE HAVE ADD A NEW NOTE  -->
  setNotes(notes.concat(noteObject))
  <!-- CLEAR THE INPUT FIELD AFTER SUBMISSION -->
   <!-- so as the input field can be clear for the user to type in something new  -->
  setNewNote('')
}

<!-- The new note is added to the list of notes using the concat array method, introduced in part 1: -->
setNotes(notes.concat(noteObject))

THAT METHOD DOESNT MUTATE the original notes array, but rather creates a new copy of the array with the  
NEW ITEM ADDED TO THE END -> WE MUST NEVER MUTATE STATE DIRECTLY 

THE EVENT HANDLER ALSO resets or clears the value of the controlled input element by calling the setNewNote function of the newNote State:
setNewNote('') 

