Let's change the component so that it stores a list of all the notes 
to be displayed in the notesToShow variable. The items on the list depend on the state of the component 

import { useState } from 'react'
import Note from './components/Note'

const App = (props) => {
  const [notes, setNotes] = useState(props.notes)
  const [newNote, setNewNote] = useState('') 
  const [showAll, setShowAll] = useState(true)

  // ...

<!-- returns a array of important notes  -->
 <!-- This variable depends on the state of the note, -->
  <!-- So it uses the notes state -->
  const notesToShow = showAll
    ? notes
    : notes.filter(note => note.important === true)

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Notes</h1>
      <ul>

        {notesToShow.map(note =>
          <Note key={note.id} note={note} />
        )}
      </ul>
      // ...
    </div>
  )
}

<!-- The definition of the notesToShow variable is rather compact -->

const notesToShow = showAll
  ? notes
  : notes.filter(note => note.important === true)

<!-- The definition uses the conditional operator also found in many other languages -->
<!-- so lets look at it in detail  -->
const result = condition ? val1 : val2

The best way to compare in JS is to use (===) instead of (==)
<!-- That comparision up there is redundant, so we simply write  -->
notes.filter(note => note.important)

So lets-> Add a functionality that enables users to toggle the showAll state of the Application 
from the user interface. The relevant changes are show below  

import { useState } from 'react' 
import Note from './components/Note'

const App = (props) => {
  const [notes, setNotes] = useState(props.notes) 
  const [newNote, setNewNote] = useState('')
  <!-- shows important notes  -->
  const [showAll, setShowAll] = useState(true)

  // ...

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Notes</h1>

      <div>
        <button onClick={() => setShowAll(!showAll)}>
          show {showAll ? 'important' : 'all'}
        </button>
      </div>
      <ul>
        {notesToShow.map(note =>
          <Note key={note.id} note={note} />
        )}
      </ul>
      // ...    
    </div>
  )
}

GETTING DATA FROM SERVER 
For a while now, we have only been on "FRONT END". i.e Client-side(browser) functionality 
We will begin with WORKING ON "BACKend" SERVER-SIDE functionality . 

<!-- WE WILL UNDERSTAND HOW THE CODE EXECUTED IN THE BROWSER COMMUNICATES WITH THE BACKEND  -->

Lets use a tool -->  JSON Server to act as our server 

LETS START the JSON Server without a separate installation 
by running the following npx command in the root directory of the application 

npx json-server --port 3001 db.json

THE IDEA will be to save the notes to the server!!
THE react Code fetches the notes from the server, which in this case means saving them to the json-server.
 The react code fetches the notes from the server and renders them to the screen !! 
 "WHENEVER A NEW NOTE IS ADDED TO THE APPLICATION, TEH REACT CODE ALSO SENDS IT TO THE SERVER TO MAKE THE  NEW  NOTE PERSIST 
 IN MEMORY"
 -> SO the json-server stores all the data in the db.json file-> which resides on the server !! 
 -> IN THE REAL WORLD, Data would be stored in some kind of database -> HOWEVER, json-server is a handy tool that enables use of server-side functionality 
 in the development phas without having to set up a real database!!
 
 Fetch method, which is based on PROMISES, instead of the event-driven modle used by XHR 

 <!-- HOW DATA WAS FETCHED USING XHR  -->

 const xhttp = new XMLHttpRequest()

xhttp.onreadystatechange = function() {
  if (this.readyState == 4 && this.status == 200) {
    const data = JSON.parse(this.responseText)
    // handle the response that is saved in variable data
  }
}

xhttp.open('GET', '/data.json', true)
xhttp.send()

<!-- THE CODE DOESNT EXECUTE SYNCHORONOUSLY BUT ASYNCHRONOUSLY -->
 JS engines are single-threaded, which means that they cannot execute code in parallel
 -So its a requirement in practice to use a non-blocking model for executing IO operations 
 -Otherwise, the browser would "freeze" during, for instance, the fetching of data from a server !! 
  -Another consquence  of this single threaded nature of JS engines is that if some code 
  execution takes up alot of time, the browser will get stuck for the duration of the execution, 

  STUDY WHAT THE HECK IS EVENT LOOK (YOUTUBE VIDEO!!)

NPM -> FETCHING DATA FROM THE SERVER 
we use the promise-based function (FETCH) TO pull data from the server 
WE WILL BE USING THE AXIOS library instead for communication between browser and server. 
It functions like fetch but is somewhat more pleasant to use 
A clear indicator that a project uses npm is the package.json file located at the root of the project. 

THE DEPENDECIES part is of most interest -> It defines what dependencies, or external libraries, the project has  
WE NEED TO USE AXIOS -> We could define the library directly in the package.json file, but its better to install it from the command line !! 

npm install axios
Its now installed in our dependencies object, but its code(code of the library is downloaded into the node_modules directory)

<!-- NOW LETS ADD ANOTHER-> install json-server as a development dependency(
 ONLY USED DURING DEVELOPMENT) -->
 npm install json-server --save-dev
 make a small addition to the scripts part of the package.json file 

 We can now conveniently, without parameter definitions, start the json-server from the project root directory with the command:
 npm run server

 AXIOS AND PROMISES 
 JSON-SERVER is assumed to be running on port 3001 

 <!-- WE CAN BRING THE LIBRARY INTO USE by import it / use the import statement -->

 <!-- The axios into use -->
 import axios from 'axios'

 <!-- a request from the browser to the server -->
const promise = axios.get('http://localhost:3001/notes')
console.log(promise)
<!-- another request from the browser to the server -->
const promise2 = axios.get('http://localhost:3001/foobar')
console.log(promise2)

<!-- THE AXIOS'S METHOD get returns a PROMISE -->
 A PROMISE IS AN OBJECT REPRESENTING THE EVENTUAL COMPLETION OR FAILURE OF AN ASYNCHRONOUS OPERATION 
A PROMISE CAN HAVE THREE STATES: PENDING, FULFILLED, REJECTED
(I) PENDING-> asynchronous operation is still in progress not yet finished and the final value isnt availabe yet 
(II) FULFILLED-> the operation has been completed successfully and the final value is available, WHICH IS GENERALLY SUCCESSFUL 
(III) REJECTED-> means that an error prevent the final value from being determined, which generally represents a FAILED OPERATION 

IF WE WANT TO ACCESS THE RESULT OF THE OPERATION represent by the promise, 
we must register an event handler to the promise. this is achieved using the method then: 
<!-- object is returned as a value from get, which is assigned to the promise variable -->
const promise = axios.get('http://localhost:3001/notes')

<!-- Access the value returned by the promise -->
promise.then(response => {
  console.log(response)
})
<!-- The returned response object contains all the essential data relate
 to the response of an HTTP GET request, which would include the returned data, status code and headers -->
 <!-- SO STORED THE PROMISE OBJECT IS UNNECESSARY SO WE chain the then method 
  call to the axios method call -->

  axios.get('http://localhost:3001/notes').then(response => {
  const notes = response.data
  console.log(notes)
})

THE DATA RETURNED by the server is plain text, Basically just 1 long STRING... 
The axios library is still able to parse the data into JS ARRAY
SINCE THE SERVER HAS SPECIFIED THAT THE DATA FORMAT IS application/json; 
using the content-type header 

<!-- WE CAN FINALLY BEGIN USING THE DATA FETCHED FROM THE SERVER -->

<!-- the below CODE has many issues, as we're rendering the entire APP 
 COMPONENT only when we successfully retrieve a response -->

