 EFFECT-HOOKS 
 We have already used the state hooks that provide state to React components 
 BUT WE WILL USE effect hooks as a new feature now 

 -> Effects let a component  connect to and synchronize with external systems .
 THIS INCLUDES dealing with network, browser DOM, animations, widgets written using a different UI library, and other non-REACT CODE 

 SO, EFFECT hooks are precisely the right tool to use when fetching data from a server 

 LETS TAKE A LOOK AT THIS CODE 

 useEffect(() => {
  console.log('effect')
  axios
    .get('http://localhost:3001/notes').then(response => {
      console.log('promise fulfilled')
      setNotes(response.data)
    })
}, [])

lets write the code a bit different  
const hook =()=>{
  console.log('effect')
  axios.get('http://localhost:3001/notes')
    .then(response => {
      console.log('promise fulfilled')
      setNotes(response.data)
    })
}

useEffect(hook,[])
It now has 2 parameters-> the effect its 
By default, effect is always run after every completed render, but you can 
choose to fire it only when certain values have changed !! 
<!-- FIRST THE COMPONENT IS MOUNTED OR RENDERED BEFORE THE EFFECT IS RUN -->
In our case, however, we only want to execute the effect along with the first render !!  

The second parameter of USEEFFECT is used to specify how often the EFFECT IS RUN !! 
IF THE second parameter is an empty array [], then the effect is only run along with the first render of the component 

.. Which parts of the code are run 
.. In what order? How often? Understanding the order of events is critical 

NOTE THAT WE COULD HAVE ALSO WRITTEN THE CODE FOR THE EFFECT FUNCTION THIS WAY 

