THE DEVELOPMENT RUNTIME ENVIRONMENT 
SUMMARY 
... The browser gets the JS from the REACT-DEV-SERVER, 
which is the application that runs after the command npm run dev . 
... tHE DEV-SERVER transforms the JS into a format understood by the browser, among other things 
it stitches  together JS FROM DIFFERENT FILES INTO ONE FILE, MORE ON DEV-SERVER in more detail (PART 7 of the course)

The React application running in the browser fetches JSON formatted data 
from json-server running on port 3001 on the machine. The server we query the data from -json-server-gets its data from the file data.json in the root of the project.

AT THIS POINT in development, all parts of the application happen to reside on the 
software developer's machine, OTHERWISE known as the localhost. The situtation changes when the application is deployed to the internet 
MORE IN PART 3 

D) ALTERING DATA IN SERVER 
When creating notes / any data in our applicatio, we would naturally want to store it in some 
backend server. 
THE JSON-SERVER package claims to be a so called  REST or RESTful API in its documentation 

HERE we will look at conventional use of routes,aka URLs and HTTP request types, in REST 

REST 
-- WE refer to individual data objects, such as the notes in our application AS RESOURCES 
-- EVERY resource has a unique address associated with it- ITS URL. 
-- So we locate an individual note at the resource URL notes/3, where 3 is the id of the resource 
-- The notes URL, on the other hand, would point to a resource collection containing  all the notes.. 

-- RESOURCES are fetched from the server with HTTP GET requests. e.g AN HTTP GET request 
to the URL notes/3 will return the note that has the id number 3. AN HTTP GET REQUEST 
to the notes URL would return a list of all notes.. 

xxxx- Creating a new resource for storing a note/ any data is done by making an 
HTTP POST request to the notes URL according to the REST convention . THE DATA FOR THE NEW 
RESOURCE IS SENT IN THE BODY OF THE REQUEST. 

ALL data is required to be sent in JSON FORMAT. This means that the data must 
be correctly formatted string and that the reques must contain the Content-Type request header with value application/json 

xx- SENDING DATA TO THE SERVER 
<!-- addNote = event => {
  event.preventDefault()
  
  THE CREATED OBJECT 
  const noteObject = {
    content: newNote,
    important: Math.random() < 0.5,
  }
 ADD IT TO THE SERVER USING AXIOS LIBRARY 
  axios
    .post('http://localhost:3001/notes', noteObject)
    .then(response => {
      console.log(response)
    })
} -->
-> We dont create an id, for the object we have sent to the SERVER because 
we let the SERVER generate ids for our resources 

--- the object is sent to the server using the axios post method. The registered event handler logs 
the response that is sent back form the server to the console 
--- The newly create note resource is stored in the value of the DATA PROPERTY of the response object.

xxx- Quite Often it is useful to inspect HTTP requests in THE NETWORK TAB of chrome dev tools.
WHICH IS HEAVILY USED AT THE BEGINNING OF PART 0...

xxxx- We can use the inspector to check that the headers sent in the POST request are what we expected them to be 
Since the data we sent in the POST request was a JS Object, axios automatically knew to set the 
appropriate application/json value for the CONTENT-TYPE HEADER. 
The tab payload can be used to check the request data. 
-ALSO THE tab RESPONSE is useful, because it shows what the data the server responded with!!

