function and passes it to every Note component 

const toggleImportanceOf = (id) => {
    console.log('importance of ' + id + ' needs to be toggled')
  }

Notice how every note receives its own unique event handler function since the id of every note is UNIQUE . 
E.G if note.id is 3, the event handler function returned by toggleImportance(note.id) will be 
() => { console.log('importance of 3 needs to be toggled') }

xxx - Individual notes stored in the JSON-SERVER backend can be modified in 2 different ways making 
HTTP requests to the note's unique URL. We can either replace the entire note with an HTTP PUT request or only 
change some of he note's properties with an HTTP PATCH request !! 

const toggleImportanceOf = id => {
  <!-- address where we are sending the reques -->
  const url = `http://localhost:3001/notes/${id}`
  <!-- Find the note with the specific id before we change it  -->
  const note = notes.find(n => n.id === id)
  <!-- Change the note's important property -->
  const changedNote = { ...note, important: !note.important }

  <!-- NOW TELL THE SERVER TO UPDATE THE NOTE -->
  axios.put(url, changedNote).then(response => {
    setNotes(notes.map(note => note.id === id ? response.data : note))
  })
}

xxx - The array find  method -> is used to find the note we want to modify, and we then assign it the note variable . 
xxx - After this, we create a new object that is an exact copy of the old note, apart from the important property that has the value flipped(from true to false or from false to true)
xxx - The code for creating the new object that uses the OBJECT SPREAD syntax may seem a bit strange at first .. 

const changedNote = { ...note, important: !note.important }
We do a so-called SHALLOW COPY, meaning hat the values of the new object are the same as the values in the old object. 

xxx - The new note is then sent with a PUT REQUEST to the backend WHERE IT WILL REPLACE THE OLD OBJECT!! 
xxx - The callback function sets the component's notes state to a new array that contains all the items 
from the previous notes array, except for the old note which is replaced by the updated version of it returned by the server!! 


axios.put(url, changedNote).then(response => {
  setNotes(notes.map(note => note.id === id ? response.data : note))
})

<!-- The map method creates a new array by mapping every item from the old array into an item n the new array 
 The NEW ARRAY IS CREATED CONDITIONALLY SO THAT if note.id === id is true; 
 the note object returned by the server is added to the array-->

 <!-- IF THE CONDITION IS FALSE, THEN WE SIMPLY copy the item from the old array into the new array instead  -->

 EXTRACTING COMMUNICATION WITH THE BACKEND INTO A SEPARATE MODULE 
 
 xxx - The App component has become bloated after adding the code for communicating with the backend server... 
 In the spirit of SINGLE RESPONSIBILITY PRINCIPLE (separation of CONCERNS), we deem it wise to extract this communication into its own MODULE 

 Lets create a src/services directory and add a file there called notes.js 

 import axios from 'axios'
<!-- Location of all the resources -->
const baseUrl = 'http://localhost:3001/notes'

<!-- Fetch all notes or resources on the server -->
const getAll = () => {
  return axios.get(baseUrl)
}

<!-- Create a new object into the server  -->
const create = newObject => {
  return axios.post(baseUrl, newObject)
}

<!-- Update an object or resource in the server  -->
const update = (id, newObject) => {
  return axios.put(`${baseUrl}/${id}`, newObject)
}

<!-- We package it in an object so as to import it to the 
 were it will be useful and were the data will be used  -->
export default { 
  getAll: getAll, 
  create: create, 
  update: update 
}

The module returns an object that has 3 functions (getAll, create, and update)
as its properties that deal with notes. The functions directly return THE PROMISES 
returned by the AXIOS methods 

The App component uses import to get access to the module 

<!-- An object is being imported into this file -->
 <!-- Each property of the object returns a promise  -->
import noteService from './services/notes'

const App = () => {

The funcions of the module can be used directly with the imported variable noteService as follows: 

const App = () => {
  // ...

  <!-- Fetch data from server  -->
  useEffect(() => {
<!-- so now lets fetch the notes so as to render them to the screen -->
    noteService
      .getAll()
      <!-- so since the promise is returned, now we need to access the values
       returned by the server -->
      .then(response => {
        setNotes(response.data)
      })
  }, [])

  <!-- Edit an individual object  with its id -->
  const toggleImportanceOf = id => {
    <!-- find the note with the id we want to edit  -->
    const note = notes.find(n => n.id === id)
    <!-- after found, edit it  -->
    const changedNote = { ...note, important: !note.important }

 <!-- Now lets tell the server to update it  -->
    noteService
      .update(id, changedNote)
      .then(response => {
        setNotes(notes.map(note => note.id === id ? response.data : note))
      })
  }

  const addNote = (event) => {
    event.preventDefault()
    <!-- create a new object  -->
    const noteObject = {
      content: newNote,
      important: Math.random() > 0.5
    }
 
   <!-- Then lets tell the server to add it into the notes  -->
    noteService
      .create(noteObject)
      .then(response => {
        setNotes(notes.concat(response.data))
        setNewNote('')
      })
  }

  // ...
}

export default App

We could take our implementation a step further. When the App component uses thhe functions, it receives an object that contains the 
entire response for the HTTP request:::

  noteService
  .getAll()
  .then(response => {
    setNotes(response.data)
  })

  The App component only uses the response.data property of the response object 

  The module would be much nicer to user if, instead of the entire HTTP response, we would 
  only get the response data. Using the module would then look like this !! 

  noteService
  .getAll()
  .then(initialNotes => {
    setNotes(initialNotes)
  })

  We can achieve this by changing the code in the modules as follows( )
  <!-- UPDATE MODULE FOR INTERACTING WITH THE SERVER -->
   import axios from 'axios'
const baseUrl = 'http://localhost:3001/notes'

const getAll = () => {
  const request = axios.get(baseUrl)
  return request.then(response => response.data)
}

const create = newObject => {
  const request = axios.post(baseUrl, newObject)
  return request.then(response => response.data)
}

const update = (id, newObject) => {
  const request = axios.put(`${baseUrl}/${id}`, newObject)
  return request.then(response => response.data)
}

export default { 
  getAll: getAll, 
  create: create, 
  update: update 
}

WE NO LONGER RETURN THE PROMISE returned by axios directly. Instead, we assign the promise to the request  variable and call its then method

<!-- Use this as an example -->
 const getAll = () => {
  const request = axios.get(baseUrl)
  return request.then(response => response.data)
}

THE MODIFIED GETALL FUNCTION STILL RETURNS A PROMISE, as the then method of a promise also returns a 
PROMISE... 
When the HTTP request is successful, the promsie returns the data sent back in the response from the backend  

<!-- SO LETS UPDATE OUR APP TO SO THAT THE CALLBACKS CAN USE DIRECTLY THE RETURNED response data -->

const App = () => {
  // ...

  useEffect(() => {
    noteService
      .getAll()
      .then(initialNotes => {
        setNotes(initialNotes)
      })
  }, [])

  const toggleImportanceOf = id => {
    const note = notes.find(n => n.id === id)
    const changedNote = { ...note, important: !note.important }

    noteService
      .update(id, changedNote)

      .then(returnedNote => {
        setNotes(notes.map(note => note.id === id ? returnedNote : note))
      })
  }

  const addNote = (event) => {
    event.preventDefault()
    const noteObject = {
      content: newNote,
      important: Math.random() > 0.5
    }

    noteService
      .create(noteObject)

      .then(returnedNote => {
        setNotes(notes.concat(returnedNote))
        setNewNote('')
      })
  }

  // ...
}

